195 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 101 0 R
/E (This is what researchers have come to call sign blindness, a phenomenon as predictable as it is underappreciated by those who design interpretive environments. When text accumulates, when warnings multiply, when every surface competes equally for notice, the brain responds not with expanded attention but with a kind of protective withdrawal. Visual clutter does not merely fail to inform. It actively diminishes the retention of everything around it, drowning signal in noise until the whole environment begins to feel like background. Wolf recognized this decades ago, and yet the instinct to add more, to say more, to cover every contingency with another label, has proven remarkably resistant to the evidence.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [16]
>>
endobj
196 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 101 0 R
/E (Cognitive psychology has long understood what institutional designers have been slower to accept: that attention is not a tap that can be turned on by demand. It is a finite, fragile, and deeply selective faculty, one that evolved not to absorb everything but to identify what matters. In the rich sensory environment of a museum, a park, or a heritage site, this selectivity becomes especially pronounced. The visitor who walks past a densely worded panel is not being careless. They are being human. Their mind has already, in fractions of a second, assessed the signal and found it insufficient to justify the cognitive cost of full engagement.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [17]
>>
endobj
197 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 101 0 R
/E ( ~11 million bits/sec)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [18]
>>
endobj
