/E (The Scarcity of Attention)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [22]
>>
endobj
202 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 102 0 R
/E (But attention is not simply rationed randomly. It flows, with surprising fidelity, toward what already matters to the person doing the attending. Falk's work on visitor identity and motivation illuminates this with particular clarity: personal relevance is not one factor among many in determining engagement. It is the precondition for engagement. Information that does not speak to a visitor's existing interests, that does not brush against something they already care about or wonder about or feel, will pass through awareness like light through glass, leaving almost no trace.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
203 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 102 0 R
/E (The Deep Logic of Relevance)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
204 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 102 0 R
/E (This is not a flaw in the visitor. It is a feature of the mind that serves us extraordinarily well in most circumstances. In a world of infinite information, the ability to filter ruthlessly for what matters is not ignorance. It is, as Girasek observed, a rational navigational strategy, the cognitive equivalent of choosing a path through wilderness. The visitor who ignores a sign about geological formations while pausing, transfixed, before an unexpected view of the valley below is not failing to learn. They are learning something, just something the institution did not plan for and perhaps cannot measure.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
205 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 102 0 R
/E (The implications for design are profound and uncomfortable. If relevance is the gateway through which all engagement must pass, then no amount of informational content, however accurate, however beautifully worded, will reach a visitor whose motivations lie elsewhere. The institution cannot manufacture relevance from the outside. It can only create conditions in which the visitor's own curiosity, already alive and already searching, finds something worth meeting.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
