<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 103 0 R
/E (Here, finally, is where the psychology of attention offers not just diagnosis but possibility. Because while attention cannot be commanded, it can be seduced. Curiosity, that most ancient and generous of human impulses, does not wait to be given permission. It reaches, spontaneously and irresistibly, toward the unexpected, the unresolved, the quietly strange. And when a space understands this, when it designs not for compliance but for interruption of the ordinary, something shifts.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
224 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 103 0 R
/E (The dinosaur sound drifting unexpectedly down a forest path. The intriguing question suspended from a tree branch where a label was never expected to be. The exhibit that begins not with an answer but with a mystery, that leans toward the visitor and whispers: did you know? These are not gimmicks. They are invitations issued in the precise language that human attention has always responded to. They work because they do not ask the visitor to abandon their own inner world and enter the institution's. They find the visitor exactly where they are and offer them something genuinely worth the turn of a head.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
225 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 103 0 R
/E (Multi-sensory cues, narrative structure, the deliberate disruption of expectation: museum educators have understood the power of these tools for generations, even if institutional design has not always trusted them enough to let them lead. The research is consistent and compelling. Exhibits that provoke questions rather than deliver answers, that engage the body as well as the mind, that tell stories with a beginning and an arc and a moment of genuine surprise, hold attention longer, generate deeper processing, and leave traces in memory that outlast the visit itself.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
