/P 104 0 R
/E (What cognitive psychology ultimately offers to the designers of informal learning spaces is not a set of tricks for capturing distracted visitors. It is something more fundamental: a truthful portrait of the mind the space was built for. That mind is not deficient. It is not lazy or indifferent or culturally impoverished. It is finite, purposeful, and exquisitely tuned to its own needs and questions.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
237 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 104 0 R
/E (To design for that mind honestly is to accept that passive content will rarely penetrate, that more information is not always more, and that the visitor's selective attention is not an obstacle between them and learning but the very mechanism through which any real learning becomes possible. The space that earns attention does so not by demanding it, but by deserving it, by offering something so genuinely curious, so unexpectedly alive, that the mind turns toward it not out of obligation, but out of the oldest human instinct there is: the desire to know.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
