<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 106 0 R
/E (Institutions write their expectations into the very grammar of their spaces. Exhibit layouts suggest sequences. Label placement implies reading order. The model patron who follows the ideal path and honors the curatorial narrative is less a description of a real human being than a projection of institutional hope. Real visitors arrive carrying their own lives: a couple celebrating an anniversary, a parent trying to spark something in a restless child, a group of friends who chose this over a dozen other ways to spend their afternoon. They enter not as students reporting for instruction but as free agents, navigating the space with their own compass.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
257 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 106 0 R
/E (Two Languages, One Room)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
258 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 106 0 R
/E (The gap between institutional expectation and visitor reality is a gap between two different understandings of what the space is for. The institution speaks the language of education and curated meaning. The visitor often speaks the language of leisure, connection, and personal discovery. Both are legitimate. But they are frequently spoken past each other, in the same room, without either party fully realizing the other is not listening.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
259 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 106 0 R
/E (Visitors consistently perceive museums and parks as leisure environments, not compulsory classrooms where disengagement carries moral weight. Skipping a panel to take a photograph is not negligence. It is a coherent response to a moment that offered something more immediately meaningful. Yet the institution has historically reached for a troubling interpretation: disengagement becomes ignorance, selective attention becomes moral failing. This judgment, embedded in the design logic of countless spaces, positions the institution as righteous and the visitor as deficient, when the more honest reading is simply that two sets of expectations met and failed to find common ground.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
