/P 107 0 R
/E (Falk's contribution is simple but quietly revolutionary. Rather than asking what visitor behavior is correct, he asks what visitors are truly seeking. Educational engagement is only one motivation among many. Visitors seek restoration, social connection, wonder, and the pleasure of time spent on their own terms. These are not lesser than the desire for learning. In many cases, they are the very conditions under which real learning becomes possible at all.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
276 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 107 0 R
/E (The tension between institutional expectation and visitor reality is not inevitable. It is a design choice that can be unmade, beginning with the willingness to ask, with genuine humility, not what visitors should be doing, but what they are actually doing and why. The institution that holds that question with curiosity rather than judgment will find, waiting in the answer, a far more honest version of itself.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
277 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 107 0 R
/E (The question of who decides what is right inside an informal learning space does not have a single answer. Authority is not held by one hand but distributed across three forces in a constant, largely unspoken negotiation.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
