296 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 108 0 R
/E (At the most visible apex stand the curators, educators, and managers. They write the labels, arrange the rooms, and decide what deserves a spotlight. Their authority is earned through expertise and genuine commitment to preservation and education. But belief, however sincere, is not the same as reality. The assumption that visitors should conform to curatorial intent quietly smuggles in a paternalism that positions the institution as the rightful author of the visitor's experience, designing not around who the visitor is, but who the institution believes they should become. It is a generous impulse, but ultimately a one-sided conversation.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
297 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 108 0 R
/E (The Pressure of the Market)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [13]
>>
endobj
298 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 108 0 R
/E (Below institutional idealism, a second force operates with rather less sentiment. The market does not care about curatorial intent. It cares about whether people come and return. Museums that once banned cameras have softened, not because their philosophy changed, but because visitor surveys made the cost of rigidity plain. Market forces accomplished what decades of visitor studies struggled to achieve: they made institutions listen. Encouraging, because visitor preferences eventually reshape institutional behavior. Unsettling, because it means the primary lever for change is economic rather than ethical.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [14]
>>
endobj
299 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 108 0 R
/E (The Visitor's Quiet Vote)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [15]
>>
endobj
300 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 108 0 R
/E (The third force is the most diffuse and most democratic. Visitors do not write policy, but their accumulated choices are irresistible. Every skipped panel, every unexpected pause, every raised camera is a vote cast without words. One national park study found that targeted, consequence-specific signage reduced dangerous behavior by sixty three percent, not because visitors were lectured more forcefully, but because the message was finally relevant enough to earn their attention. The visitor's vote is not opposition. It is an offer of collaboration, if only the institution is willing to read it honestly.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [16]
>>
endobj
