322 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 109 0 R
/E (The triangle above maps the three forces. Now here is how their authority actually breaks down and who has historically dominated.)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [18]
>>
endobj
323 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 109 0 R
/E (A Triangle in Need of Rebalancing)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [19]
>>
endobj
324 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 109 0 R
/E (Curators hold formal authority. Markets hold economic authority. Visitors hold behavioral authority. For most of history, the first has dominated, the second has occasionally disrupted, and the third has been noted only when impossible to ignore. A more honest model would begin with who the visitor already is, treat market signals as democratic feedback, and recognize the visitor's quiet accumulated choices as the most truthful data the institution will ever receive. Authority, in the end, is most legitimate when held lightly, when it invites rather than instructs, and when it remains genuinely curious about the people it was built to serve.)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [20]
>>
endobj
