357 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 112 0 R
/E (REFRAMING THE VISITOR CONTRACT)
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
358 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 112 0 R
/E (The central provocation of this paper has been to challenge a deeply embedded institutional assumption: that visitors who do not read every label or absorb every piece of interpretive content are somehow failing in their role. This framing imposes a behavioral contract on visitors that was never negotiated and is, as the evidence demonstrates, fundamentally at odds with how human cognition actually operates.)
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
359 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 112 0 R
/E (Humans are informavores by nature, curiosity-driven, and wired to pursue information that is novel, relevant, and personally meaningful. Yet the modern world now floods those same cognitive systems with volumes of information that no single mind was ever built to absorb. What emerges from this collision is not apathy, but adaptation. Selective attention and goal-directed behavior are the quiet, rational responses of a finite being learning to navigate an infinite stream. When a visitor walks past an interpretive panel without pausing, they are not failing the space. They are listening to something else, something that called to them first.)
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
360 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 112 0 R
/E (The institutional habit of defining "proper" visitor behavior reflects a didactic model that has long privileged the institution's voice over the visitor's inner life. Perhaps this posture once made sense, in quieter times when knowledge was scarce, and a museum wall held something the world outside could not offer. But that world has dissolved. To keep designing for a visitor conditioned to passively receive curated wisdom is to design for a ghost.)
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
361 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 112 0 R
/E (What is needed, then, is not a lower standard for visitors but a deeper imagination in design. Interpretive content must earn its place in the visitor's awareness, not by demanding attention as a duty, but by meeting visitors where their curiosity already lives. And institutions must grow more honest about what learning truly means in these spaces. A family that spends an afternoon in shared laughter and wandering wonder, having read perhaps only a handful of signs, may leave carrying something far more lasting than any fact. Learning here is not the transfer of information from wall to mind. It is the slow construction of meaning, woven from emotion, memory, and human presence.)
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
362 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 112 0 R
/E (In an age of relentless information, the most generous thing an informal learning space can offer is not more content, but a gentler contract: one that treats its visitors as intelligent, self-directed souls whose attention is not owed, but earned, and whose simplest moments of joy are not failures of engagement, but its quietest, most honest form.)
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
